&lt;h2 id=&quot;what-is-volume&quot;&gt;What is Volume?&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Volume is simply the &lt;strong&gt;number of shares traded&lt;/strong&gt; during a given period — a day, an hour, a candle.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If 50 lakh shares of Reliance changed hands today, today&#39;s volume is 50,00,000. That&#39;s it. No formula, no settings, no interpretation needed for the raw number — volume is one of the purest data points on your entire chart.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Volume is displayed at the bottom of most charts as vertical bars:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Tall bar&lt;/strong&gt; = lots of shares traded that period&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Short bar&lt;/strong&gt; = few shares traded that period&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Bars are usually colored to match the candle: green bar = volume on an up-candle, red bar = volume on a down-candle&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here&#39;s the crucial insight: volume measures &lt;strong&gt;activity and conviction.&lt;/strong&gt; A price move backed by heavy volume means large numbers of traders — including big institutions — participated with real money. A move on thin volume means very few people were actually involved.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2 id=&quot;why-matters&quot;&gt;Why Volume Matters: The Truth Serum&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Think of price and volume like a courtroom trial:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Price is the witness&#39;s statement.&lt;/strong&gt; Anyone can claim anything — &quot;the stock is going to the moon!&quot;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Volume is the evidence.&lt;/strong&gt; It shows whether real money actually backed that claim.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A price move on heavy volume is a statement backed by evidence. A price move on weak volume is an unverified claim — it can be contradicted at any moment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Volume reveals conviction.&lt;/strong&gt; Big players — mutual funds, FIIs, institutions — cannot hide their footprints. When they buy or sell in size, volume explodes. You may not know &lt;em&gt;who&lt;/em&gt; is trading, but volume always shows you &lt;em&gt;how much&lt;/em&gt; — and that&#39;s often enough.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is also why volume is unique among all the tools on your chart: moving averages, RSI, and MACD are all &lt;em&gt;calculated from price&lt;/em&gt;. Volume is independent — a second, separate stream of truth. When price and volume tell the same story, your probability goes up. When they disagree, that disagreement itself is information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2 id=&quot;reading-bars&quot;&gt;How to Read Volume Bars&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before decoding price-volume combinations, you need a baseline. Raw volume numbers mean nothing without context:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;Step 1: Compare to Average Volume&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most charting platforms display a volume average line (typically a 20-period average). Ask with every candle: &lt;strong&gt;is current volume above or below the recent average?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th scope=&quot;col&quot;&gt;Volume Level&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th scope=&quot;col&quot;&gt;Meaning&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Well above average&lt;/strong&gt; (1.5x–3x+)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;High conviction move — strong participation, worth trusting&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Roughly average&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Normal market activity — neutral signal&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Well below average&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Low interest — weak conviction, move is fragile&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;Step 2: Match Volume Bars to Their Candles&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A green candle with a tall volume bar means buyers didn&#39;t just push price up — they pushed it up with serious force. A green candle with a tiny volume bar means price rose, but barely anyone participated. Same candle, entirely different meaning.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;Step 3: Watch Volume Over a Trend&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Zoom out. In healthy uptrends, up-days typically carry more volume than down-days. When that relationship flips — down-days start out-volume up-days — the smart money is quietly exiting before price shows it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2 id=&quot;four-combos&quot;&gt;The 4 Price-Volume Combinations (Core of Everything)&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Everything in volume analysis flows from four combinations. Master this table and you can decode any market situation:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;Combination 1: Price Up + Volume Up (Strongest Bullish Signal)&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Buyers are aggressive &lt;strong&gt;and&lt;/strong&gt; fully committed. Large money is entering. This is the healthiest uptrend behavior — rallies powered by real demand.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What to do:&lt;/strong&gt; Trend is trustworthy. Hold longs, buy pullbacks, and treat this trend with respect until this relationship breaks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;Combination 2: Price Down + Volume Up (Strong Selling Pressure)&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sellers are aggressive and committed. Real distribution is happening — big players are unloading. Strong down-volume on break of support confirms genuine weakness.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What to do:&lt;/strong&gt; Respect the weakness. Don&#39;t buy dips that fall on rising volume — that&#39;s catching a falling knife with witnesses.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;Combination 3: Price Up + Volume Down (Suspicious Rally — Warning)&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the quiet warning professional traders never ignore. Price is rising, but participation is shrinking — fewer and fewer people are driving the move.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What it means:&lt;/strong&gt; The rally is running on fumes. Often this is the final surge of a trend — late buyers, thin commitment, no institutional fuel left. It frequently appears at market tops as &quot;distribution in disguise.&quot;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What to do:&lt;/strong&gt; Tighten stop losses on longs. Don&#39;t start fresh buying into a rally that volume doesn&#39;t confirm.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;Combination 4: Price Down + Volume Down (Weak Selling — Potential Reversal Setup)&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Price is falling, but almost nobody is selling aggressively. The sellers are exhausted — declines driven by absence of buyers, not presence of sellers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What it means:&lt;/strong&gt; Often marks the late stage of a downtrend. When price falls but volume dries up, selling pressure is burning out — a bottom may be forming. This is exactly when you watch for reversal candles at support.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What to do:&lt;/strong&gt; Put the stock on your watchlist. Falling-volume declines into support + a high-volume reversal candle = one of the best bottom-fishing setups in trading.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;The Complete Picture&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th scope=&quot;col&quot;&gt;Price&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th scope=&quot;col&quot;&gt;Volume&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th scope=&quot;col&quot;&gt;Signal&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th scope=&quot;col&quot;&gt;Action&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;⬆ Rising&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;⬆ Rising&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Healthy uptrend&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Hold / buy pullbacks&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;⬇ Falling&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;⬆ Rising&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Genuine selling&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Stay out / respect weakness&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;⬆ Rising&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;⬇ Falling&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Weak rally (warning)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Tighten stops, don&#39;t chase&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;⬇ Falling&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;⬇ Falling&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Exhausted sellers&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Watch for reversal at support&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The golden question for every trade:&lt;/strong&gt; &quot;Is volume confirming my idea?&quot; If you&#39;re buying, you want up-moves on volume. If you&#39;re shorting, you want down-moves on volume. If volume disagrees with your trade — you&#39;re the one disagreeing with the market.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2 id=&quot;breakouts&quot;&gt;Volume and Breakouts: Catching Fakeouts&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is where volume analysis pays for itself. Remember the fakeout scenario from the beginning of this article? Here&#39;s the professional&#39;s checklist for telling real breakouts from traps:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;A Genuine Breakout Looks Like This:&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Volume at least 1.5x–2x the average&lt;/strong&gt; — ideally 2–3x&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A &lt;strong&gt;strong candle closing beyond the level&lt;/strong&gt; — not a wick poking through&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Follow-through volume on the next 1–2 candles&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Retest of the broken level on &lt;strong&gt;lower volume&lt;/strong&gt; (sellers absent), followed by fresh rise&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;A Suspect Breakout Looks Like This:&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Price crosses the level on &lt;strong&gt;average or below-average volume&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Small, indecisive candles around the breakout&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No follow-through — next candle falls back inside the range&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Volume spikes only on the &lt;em&gt;rejection&lt;/em&gt; candle back down (the real money was selling)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why does this work?&lt;/strong&gt; A true breakout must consume all the sell orders sitting at the resistance level. That requires massive buying — which necessarily shows up as massive volume. A breakout on thin volume simply means nobody bothered to sell &lt;em&gt;yet&lt;/em&gt; — the moment real sellers appear, the move collapses.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Real example structure:&lt;/strong&gt; A stock trades in a range between ₹500 and ₹600 for two months. It finally closes at ₹615 on volume 2.5x average — genuine breakout, worth trading. Compare: the same stock closes at ₹610 on volume 40% &lt;em&gt;below&lt;/em&gt; average — statistically, that breakout fails far more often. Same chart. Same level. Completely different probability — all because of one bottom-of-the-chart detail.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2 id=&quot;spikes&quot;&gt;Volume Spikes: Reading Extreme Events&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sometimes volume doesn&#39;t just rise — it explodes, 5x, 10x, 20x the average. These spikes carry specific meanings:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;Common Causes of Volume Spikes&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Results and earnings announcements:&lt;/strong&gt; Quarterly results create the highest-volume days of the quarter&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Block and bulk deals:&lt;/strong&gt; Institutions buying or selling large chunks&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Index inclusion/exclusion:&lt;/strong&gt; When a stock joins the Nifty 50, index funds must buy it — massive automatic volume&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;News events:&lt;/strong&gt; M&amp;amp;A announcements, management changes, regulatory actions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Capitulation and euphoria:&lt;/strong&gt; Panic bottoms and blow-off tops&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;How to Trade Around Spikes&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Be careful with a subtle trap here: a giant green volume bar on a huge up-move looks bullish — but if it&#39;s a &lt;strong&gt;exhaustion spike&lt;/strong&gt; (news-driven climax), the move often reverses immediately. Everyone who wanted to buy has now bought — who&#39;s left to push it higher?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Practical rule:&lt;/strong&gt; After an extreme volume spike, wait 2–3 candles before trading in the spike&#39;s direction. If the move holds its ground on normal volume, it was genuine accumulation. If it gives back the move, the spike was exhaustion.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2 id=&quot;obv&quot;&gt;On-Balance Volume (OBV): The Smart Money Detector&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;OBV is the most famous volume-based indicator — and its logic is brilliantly simple.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;How OBV Works&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If today closes &lt;strong&gt;higher&lt;/strong&gt; than yesterday → add today&#39;s entire volume to OBV&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If today closes &lt;strong&gt;lower&lt;/strong&gt; than yesterday → subtract today&#39;s entire volume&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The running total creates a line — and here&#39;s the magic: &lt;strong&gt;OBV often moves before price.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;The Two OBV Signals That Matter&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. OBV Rising While Price Moves Sideways or Falls = Accumulation&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Someone is quietly absorbing all the selling without letting the price rise yet. This &quot;smart money footprint&quot; often precedes breakouts. Price catches down to what volume already knew.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. OBV Falling While Price Moves Sideways or Rises = Distribution&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Someone is unloading stock into strength — selling on every up-day — without letting the price collapse yet. This frequently warns of coming declines.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;OBV is like watching the water level behind a dam. The surface (price) looks calm — but OBV shows whether the pressure (money flow) is building or draining underneath.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;How to Use OBV Practically&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Confirm breakouts: a price breakout &lt;strong&gt;accompanied by an OBV breakout&lt;/strong&gt; is meaningfully more reliable&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Spot divergences: price makes a new high, OBV doesn&#39;t → buying pressure didn&#39;t support the new high → warning&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Keep it simple: use OBV&#39;s direction over weeks — not its day-to-day wiggles&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2 id=&quot;vwap&quot;&gt;VWAP: The Institutional Benchmark&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;VWAP (Volume Weighted Average Price)&lt;/strong&gt; is the average price of a stock today — weighted by how many shares traded at each price. Unlike a normal average that treats all prices equally, VWAP answers: &lt;em&gt;&quot;What was the average price people actually paid today?&quot;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;Why Traders Obsess Over VWAP&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Institutional traders — mutual funds, FIIs — are often judged on whether they bought &lt;em&gt;better than VWAP&lt;/em&gt;. Buy below VWAP, and you got a better-than-average price. This makes VWAP a magnet and a battlefield for the biggest players in the market.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;The Practical VWAP Rules&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Price above VWAP = buyers in control&lt;/strong&gt; for the day. Dips toward VWAP often find buying support.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Price below VWAP = sellers in control.&lt;/strong&gt; Rallies toward VWAP often meet selling.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt; VWAP acts as dynamic intraday support/resistance&lt;/strong&gt; — especially in the first hour and the last hour.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Professional intraday filter: &lt;strong&gt;take longs only above VWAP, shorts only below it.&lt;/strong&gt; This single rule keeps you trading on the right side of institutional money flow.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;One Crucial VWAP Warning&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;VWAP resets every market open — it is &lt;strong&gt;an intraday tool only.&lt;/strong&gt; Never use yesterday&#39;s VWAP line as tomorrow&#39;s support. For swing traders, VWAP&#39;s daily values can be informative, but the classic VWAP trade belongs to intraday charts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2 id=&quot;climax&quot;&gt;Volume Climax and Exhaustion&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Markets don&#39;t turn on random days — they turn on &lt;strong&gt;volume extremes.&lt;/strong&gt; There are two classic climax patterns worth knowing:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;Selling Climax (The Panic Bottom)&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After a long decline, one final day of &lt;strong&gt;massive volume&lt;/strong&gt; — 5–10x average — where price crashes and then &lt;strong&gt;recovers dramatically&lt;/strong&gt;, closing well off the lows, often with a long lower wick.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The story:&lt;/strong&gt; The last fearful holders panic-sell everything at once. But the flood of shares is met by patient buyers who absorb it all. The sellers are now empty — everyone who wanted out is out. This &quot;capitulation&quot; day is frequently THE bottom.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;Buying Climax (The Euphoric Top)&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The mirror image: after a strong rally, one explosive high-volume day where price surges and then &lt;strong&gt;closes weakly&lt;/strong&gt; — well off the highs, often with a long upper wick.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The story:&lt;/strong&gt; The last greedy buyers pile in at any price. Their buying is absorbed by patient sellers unloading into the frenzy. The buyers are now full — everyone who wanted in is in. Nothing left to fuel the rally.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How to trade climaxes:&lt;/strong&gt; Never trade the climax candle itself in real-time — you can&#39;t know it&#39;s a climax until it completes. Instead, wait for the &lt;em&gt;next&lt;/em&gt; candle to confirm the reversal, and enter with a stop beyond the climax extreme.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2 id=&quot;mistakes&quot;&gt;Common Mistakes to Avoid&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Reading volume in isolation.&lt;/strong&gt; Volume means nothing without price context. Tall red bars during a shakeout before a rally and tall red bars during a genuine crash look identical on the volume pane — price tells them apart.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Expecting volume confirmation on every move.&lt;/strong&gt; Some moves happen on normal volume — that&#39;s fine. You&#39;re looking for &lt;em&gt;divergences and extremes&lt;/em&gt;: breakouts on weak volume, breakdowns on strong volume, rallies on dying volume.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Comparing volume across different stocks.&lt;/strong&gt; A &quot;low volume&quot; day for Reliance might be a spectacular volume day for a small-cap. Always compare a stock&#39;s volume to &lt;strong&gt;its own&lt;/strong&gt; recent average.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Ignoring volume on your entry candle.&lt;/strong&gt; Your trade entry is a mini-breakout too. If your signal candle forms on weak volume, your edge is thinner than it looks.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Trading climax candles live.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Using VWAP on swing trades.&lt;/strong&gt; VWAP resets daily. Using it as multi-day support is a category error — use moving averages or horizontal levels instead.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Forgetting delivery vs total volume.&lt;/strong&gt; High volume with mostly intraday square-offs behaves differently than high volume with actual delivery buying. On swing trades, check delivery percentage data — rising delivery % on up-days signals genuine investment interest.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2 id=&quot;faqs&quot;&gt;Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;Q1. What is a good volume for a breakout?&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As a rule of thumb, a genuine breakout should show volume at least &lt;strong&gt;1.5x to 2x the 20-day average volume&lt;/strong&gt; — ideally more. The stronger the volume, the more real money consumed the resistance level. Breakouts on below-average volume fail at a noticeably higher rate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;Q2. Is high volume good or bad for a stock?&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Neither — it depends on direction and context. High volume on rising price = strength. High volume on falling price = weakness. High volume on a climax candle at an extreme = potential reversal. Volume measures conviction; price determines its meaning.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;Q3. Can I use volume analysis for intraday trading?&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes — it&#39;s essential for intraday. Key intraday volume tools: VWAP (institutional benchmark), volume spikes at open, and relative volume of breakouts from opening ranges. Intraday volume patterns are noisier, though, so always combine with price levels.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;Q4. What does volume tell you that price doesn&#39;t?&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Volume tells you &lt;strong&gt;conviction and participation&lt;/strong&gt;. Price shows the outcome; volume shows the effort behind it. Price can be manipulated by thin markets briefly — but faking sustained, heavy volume across days is nearly impossible. That&#39;s why pros trust it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;Q5. Is low volume bullish or bearish?&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On its own — neither. Low volume on a pullback in an uptrend is &lt;strong&gt;bullish&lt;/strong&gt; (sellers aren&#39;t serious). Low volume on a rally is &lt;strong&gt;bearish&lt;/strong&gt; (buyers aren&#39;t committed). Always pair the volume reading with price direction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;Q6. What is relative volume (RVOL)?&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;RVOL compares current volume to the same time period&#39;s average — e.g., &quot;this stock has already traded 3x its usual 10 AM volume.&quot; A reading above 2 signals unusual early interest — often the precursor to a significant move. Many professional screeners rank stocks by RVOL daily.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;Final Words&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Volume analysis won&#39;t give you buy and sell signals on its own — no tool does that. What it gives you is something rarer: &lt;strong&gt;a lie detector.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Price will lie to you. Fake breakouts, false rallies, headfake dumps — price paints all of them beautifully. But volume struggles to lie. Every share traded is a real transaction by a real participant with real money on the line — and patterns of real money are hard to fake.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So build this habit starting today:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Before every trade, glance at the bottom of the chart.&lt;/strong&gt; Ask: is volume confirming this move — or contradicting it? One glance. Two seconds. It might be the highest-paying habit you ever build.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your homework: open the chart of any stock that recently broke out of a range. Compare the volume on breakout day versus the average. Then find a breakout that failed — and check its volume. Do this five times, and you&#39;ll never look at a breakout the same way again.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Price tells the story. Volume tells you if it&#39;s true. 🚀&lt;/p&gt;

