                    &lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The Lost Tomb of Jesus&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
                    &lt;td&gt;Discovery Channel&lt;/td&gt;
                    &lt;td&gt;The Biblical Counterpart. While Lost Tomb deals with Biblical archaeology, Saqqara Tomb deals with the Ancient Egyptian religion. Both focus on the &quot;sealed for 2000 years&quot; tension.&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;/tr&gt;
                &lt;tr&gt;
                    &lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Tutankhamun: The Last Exhibition&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
                    &lt;td&gt;UK / Channel 4&lt;/td&gt;
                    &lt;td&gt;The Boy King Rival. This documentary defined the style of &quot;high-tech scanning of a famous mummy.&quot; Saqqara Tomb takes that technology and applies it to a lesser-known, arguably more fascinating family unit.&lt;/td&gt;
